nships (3799)]\\n\\nRECOVERY’s broader description reinforces this expectation by noting that discomfort and limitations should progressively improve and that fatigue/weakness are also typical and generally lessen over time. [Data: Entities (2200)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Return to normal activities is a key functional milestone, but timing is variable\",\n      \"explanation\": \"NORMAL ACTIVITIES is connected to RECOVERY through the idea that returning to usual daily functioning may be possible after a few weeks, depending on progress. This frames recovery not only as symptom resolution but as restoration of day-to-day capabilities. [Data: Entities (2248); Relationships (3897)]\\n\\nThe RECOVERY description further emphasizes that resuming normal routines can be relatively quick after minor interventions but may take weeks to months after major surgery, aligning the functional milestone with procedural intensity and individual healing trajectories. [Data: Entities (2200)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Rest is a defined, near-term recovery action after less extensive procedures\",\n      \"explanation\": \"REST is explicitly described as a recommended action after TURBT, noting that most people can go home the same day but need a few days to rest. This specifies a concrete behavioral recommendation within the recovery process for a less extensive procedure context. [Data: Entities (5900); Relationships (13407)]\\n\\nREST’s relationship to RECOVERY supports the interpretation that the community includes both medical course information and actionable guidance (at least for some procedures) on immediate post-treatment steps. [Data: Relationships (13407); Entities (2200)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Mentoring is framed as a coping/support strategy, especially in the context of anal cancer survivorship\",\n      \"explanation\": \"MENTORING appears as a support activity recommended for coping with anal cancer, specifically noting rarity and stigma as motivating factors for seeking and providing peer support. This introduces a psychosocial support layer to the RECOVERY-centered community. [Data: Entities (6105)]\\n\\nThe relationships further connect MENTORING to RECOVERY by advising that after recovery, a person should “pay it forward” by mentoring someone else—explicitly turning recovery into a trigger for reciprocal support behavior. [Data: Relationships (13987); Entities (2200, 6105)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Michael is the only named individual and serves as an advocate for mentoring before and after recovery\",\n      \"explanation\": \"MICHAEL is described as an anal cancer survivor who advises patients to find a mentor and later mentor others after recovery. This makes Michael the sole explicit personal voice in the network and anchors mentoring guidance in lived-experience testimony. [Data: Entities (6104); Relationships (13985)]\\n\\nMICHAEL’s tie to MENTORING (and MENTORING’s tie to RECOVERY) positions him as an influencer within this small community of concepts, emphasizing peer guidance as part of coping and post-recovery support. [Data: Relationships (13985, 13987); Entities (6104, 6105, 2200)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 2.2,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"Impact severity is low because the community is informational and support-oriented (recovery expectations, rest, and mentoring) with no provided evidence of illegality, malign intent, or operational threat.\"\n}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1604, "prompt_tokens": 2891, "total_tokens": 4495, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 150, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": n